  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nA stubborn stationary front remains parked across the region through \nMonday. Daily chances for showers and thunderstorms will be locked \nin place as rounds of disturbances move through. The main threat \nthis weekend and Monday will be flash flooding. Waterlogged soils, \nhigh PW values, and rounds of heavy rainfall will lead to scattered \ninstances of flash flooding across the area.\n\n1-2\" of rainfall will be common between now and Monday night, with \nthe northeastern portions of the state seeing higher amounts over 2 \ninches. Locally higher amounts are expected where storms train, \nwhich will likely be somewhere in southeastern OH and northern and \ncentral WV. These are the areas where chances for more considerable \nflash flooding is possible. Portions of southeast OH and northern WV \nhave been outlooked with a rare moderate risk (level 3/4) for \nexcessive rainfall today and Sunday. A slight risk (level 2/4) has \nbeen stamped across the rest of the area. A flood watch is \nsubsequently in effect until 8 AM Monday. Some counties across the \nsouthern coalfields and northeastern KY were added given a more \nsouthward trend of showers and storms in the models.\n\nCurrent model guidance shows a couple complexes of storms traversing \nIN/OH. These will eventually reach us and move across the area \nthis afternoon. Another more robust round is expected sometime\ntonight. Heavy downpours leading to flash flooding will of \ncourse be the main threat today, but damaging winds will also be\na concern with a conditional severe weather risk. Severe \nweather will ultimately depend on cloud cover and lingering \nscattered showers. How this evolves will depend on how far north\nthe stationary front moves today. It is currently remaining \nparked across our southern coalfields. However, a meso-low \nanchored along the stationary boundary will create a favorable \nwind environment for a tornado or two to occur in more \norganized, clear-air cells. This risk is more of concern for \nportions of southeastern OH, northeast KY, and far western WV.\n\nThere is still some difference in models as to how this event\nwill evolve. The ~12z HRRR shows the first MCS dropping into our\nsoutheast OH counties and continuing ESE across central and\nnorthern WV between 10 PM and 2 AM tonight. This solution shows\na quick moving, progressive bowing system with additional rounds\nof heavy showers and storms in its wake. The ~12z NAM shows a \nlater arrival (after midnight) and a less progressive, intense \nline of storms. It does show more coverage and rounds of \nadditional showers and thunderstorms behind the main line\nthat would continue through Sunday morning. Nonetheless, tonight\nand Sunday morning will likely be the period of most concern \nfor flash flooding.\n\nDepending on clearing and exit of morning activity, scattered \nshowers and storms are once again likely Sunday afternoon and\nevening with the steadfast sultry airmass in place. More of the\nsame is expected on Monday ahead of a cold front that crosses\nMonday night. This front will give us a brief break Tuesday\nthrough some of Wednesday afternoon. Models greatly diverge \nbeyond this time frame, but surface high pressure looks to hang\naround through the rest of the week into the weekend, even with\na crossing cold front Wednesday into Thursday. Allowed some\nchances for shower/storms with this front Wednesday into\nThursday and beyond with passing disturbances.",
